# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Spacedust — environment variables
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the blanks.
#
# 🔒 SECURITY: every variable below WITHOUT the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix stays on
#    the server. Never prefix an API key with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that ships it to
#    every browser. Spacedust never asks for your private key or seed phrase.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Token data source (choose one — both are free) ──────────────────────────
#
# OPTION A — no account, no key, zero cost (default)
#   Leave HELIUS_API_KEY blank. Spacedust scans with core Solana RPC using
#   getTokenAccountsByOwner for BOTH the Token and Token-2022 programs, and
#   takes names/symbols/logos from Jupiter. Fully functional.
#   Trade-off: slower on wallets holding hundreds of tokens, and the public
#   RPC endpoint is rate-limited per IP.
#
# OPTION B — faster and more robust (still free, needs a free signup, no card)
#   Add a free Helius key below. Spacedust then uses the DAS indexer, which is
#   substantially faster and returns richer metadata.
#   https://helius.dev — ~50,000 credits/day on the free tier.
HELIUS_API_KEY=

# Public RPC used when HELIUS_API_KEY is not set. Leave blank to use the
# official public endpoint (rate-limited per IP — fine for local use).
# You can also point this at any free provider: QuickNode, Alchemy, Chainstack.
# ⚠️  This value is public. Never put an API key or token in this URL; anything
#     here is shipped to every browser. Use HELIUS_API_KEY, which stays on the
#     server, for a keyed provider.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FALLBACK_RPC_URL=https://api.mainnet-beta.solana.com


# ── Jupiter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional but strongly recommended. Jupiter works keyless at 0.5 requests/sec
# which is fine for personal use, but a free key raises the limit and avoids
# rate-limit errors. Get one at https://portal.jup.ag  →  "Get API Key" (Free).
#
# If this is empty, Spacedust falls back to the keyless endpoint.
JUPITER_API_KEY=


# ── Integrator fees ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The wallet that receives Spacedust's fees. **Optional** — leave it blank and
# the app charges nothing anywhere.
#
# ⚠️  Upgrading from an older checkout? The variable used to be called
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_DONATION_WALLET, and the old name is now ignored — not
#     deprecated with a warning, simply not read. If fees stopped being charged
#     after an update, this is almost certainly why: rename the variable. An
#     unset wallet disables every fee silently, by design, so nothing complains.
#
# This must be a NEXT_PUBLIC_ value, and that is safe: it is a *receiving*
# address, and it is visible on chain anyway. It has to be public because The
# Void's fee is a SOL transfer the browser builds, and the browser cannot read a
# server-only variable.
#
# ⚠️  Never type an exchange deposit address here unless you control it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FEE_WALLET=
#
# There is one fee, and it is on rent: 1.5% of whatever any flow reclaims —
# Void sends, empty-account closes, Payload sends. It arrives as a plain SOL
# transfer the browser builds, so this wallet needs no token accounts and no
# setup beyond existing. Swaps are not charged.
#
# The wallet does have to be *funded*: a fee this small cannot create an account,
# and Solana refuses to send to an address below the rent-exempt minimum. An
# unfunded wallet makes every flow run fee-free rather than fail on chain.
#
# Check /api/health: it reports whether this wallet is set and funded.


# ── Fee sponsor (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A small hot wallet that pays the *network* fee when the user's wallet has none
# — the wallet that has rent to reclaim and no SOL to reclaim it with. It is the
# only key Spacedust ever holds, and it can do exactly one thing.
#
# How it is held: server-only, never in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, never logged.
# lib/sponsor/config.ts imports `server-only`, so importing it from client code
# fails the build instead of shipping the key.
#
# How it is spent: the browser builds an ordinary plan with this wallet as the fee
# payer and a repayment transfer at the end; the wallet signs; /api/sponsor
# re-derives every fact from those bytes (fee payer, no other appearance, no
# account creation, a repayment that covers the fee, compute budget within
# policy), simulates, and only then adds its signature. See lib/sponsor/policy.ts
# for the full rule set and its tests.
#
# How to hold it safely: treat the float as money already spent. 0.05 SOL is the
# intended float and covers thousands of transactions — each one pays the fee
# (~10,500 lamports for two signatures) and is repaid with a 2,000-lamport margin
# on top, so a healthy run grows the float instead of spending it. Top it up by
# hand if you want more headroom, and rotate it by replacing this value — there is
# no state to migrate. Signing stops entirely below
# MIN_SPONSOR_BALANCE_LAMPORTS (0.01 SOL), so the worst case is bounded by what
# the wallet holds.
#
# Format: base58 (what a wallet exports) or the 64-number JSON array
# `solana-keygen` writes. Leave blank to disable fee advances: every flow still
# works, and a wallet with no SOL is told the exact amount to send instead.
#
# ⚠️  Generate it fresh. Never reuse a wallet that holds anything else.
FEE_SPONSOR_SECRET_KEY=


# ── App behaviour ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The site's own origin, without a trailing slash. Used for `metadataBase` (so
# the generated social-preview card resolves to an absolute URL) and for
# `sitemap.xml`. Defaults to https://spacedust.cc, so a deployment that never sets
# this still ships correct links rather than ones pointing at localhost.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://spacedust.cc

# Optional branding / links shown in the footer. Safe to expose publicly.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_URL is read by three places, not just the footer: the
# footer link, the "we only post from @handle" line on /security, and the
# twitter:site attribution on the preview card. It must be an x.com or
# twitter.com profile — anything else is ignored rather than published as ours.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL appears in the footer as a mailto link, and only if it
# contains an "@". Leave it blank and the footer says nothing about contact rather
# than showing a link that silently does nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=Spacedust
NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_URL=https://x.com/spacedustpro
# The public repository, shown as "Source" in the footer — the link somebody
# clicks when they want to check that the app is what it claims to be. Only a
# github.com URL with an owner and a repository is accepted; anything else is
# ignored rather than published under that label.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=

# ── Legal / privacy contact ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only values: they are rendered into the legal pages, not bundled into
# client JavaScript. (The NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_* variants are also accepted for
# compatibility.)
#
# LEGAL_ENTITY is the legal name of the person or company that operates
# Spacedust. LEGAL_EMAIL must be a real monitored mailbox: it is the required
# privacy contact and the page warns until it is set.
#
# LEGAL_ADDRESS is optional for a hobby project. Add a real service/trading
# address when you operate as a sole trader or company if consumer/trader rules
# require one; do not publish a home address just to fill this field.
#
# LEGAL_EMAIL falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL when blank.
LEGAL_ENTITY=Spacedust
LEGAL_ADDRESS=
LEGAL_EMAIL=
# The law that governs the terms, e.g. England and Wales. Change this if the
# operator is established elsewhere.
LEGAL_JURISDICTION=England and Wales


# ── WalletConnect (phone wallets, optional) ──────────────────────────────────
# Adds a "WalletConnect" row that reaches Phantom, Solflare and hundreds more
# over the relay — and on a phone that is the only route that works from a
# normal mobile browser.
# Free project id from https://dashboard.reown.com (WalletConnect / Reown) —
# sign up, create a project, paste the Project ID here.
#
# Leave blank to skip it: the Jupiter browser extension and every other wallet
# work without this, since the extension is detected via Wallet Standard.
#
# ⚠️  On a phone this is the ONLY path that works from a normal mobile browser.
#     Mobile browsers have no wallet extensions, so nothing gets injected and
#     Phantom/Solflare only work from their own in-app browsers. WalletConnect
#     needs no in-app browser — but it does need a project id.
#
# ⚠️  It also needs HTTPS. WalletConnect derives its encryption key with
#     `crypto.subtle`, which browsers only expose in a secure context. A bare
#     LAN origin like http://192.168.1.236:3000 is NOT a secure context, so the
#     connection dies before it reaches the wallet. `localhost` counts as
#     secure; for a phone, tunnel it:
#         npx cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3000
#     (AppKit builds metadata.url from the current origin, so the tunnel works
#     as-is — but add that domain in the Reown dashboard too, or it shows a
#     domain-unverified banner.)
NEXT_PUBLIC_REOWN_PROJECT_ID=

# ── Shared rate-limit counter (optional, recommended) ────────────────────────
# The limiter in lib/api/rate-limit.ts counts per serverless instance, so what
# the deployment really serves is that budget times however many instances
# Vercel starts. These two switch on a counter every instance shares, applied by
# middleware.ts to every /api/ route.
#
# Any Redis-compatible HTTP store works; Upstash's free tier is the usual choice
# (it speaks HTTP, which is what a serverless function needs — no connection
# pool, no cold-start handshake). Copy the REST URL and token from the store.
#
# Leave both blank and nothing changes: unconfigured means every check passes.
# A store that is unreachable also passes, deliberately — a counter that hiccups
# must not take the API down with it.
#
# If you connect the store through Vercel's Upstash integration instead of
# copying values by hand, you do NOT need to fill these in: the code reads the
# names that integration creates, as-is. In precedence order —
#
#   RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL / RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_TOKEN
#   RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_KV_REST_API_URL / RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_KV_REST_API_TOKEN
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL / UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN
#   KV_REST_API_URL / KV_REST_API_TOKEN
#
# The `redis://` connection strings the integration also creates are deliberately
# not read: this client speaks HTTP, which is what a serverless function needs.
# `/api/health` reports which pair answered.
RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL=
RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_TOKEN=


# ── Content-Security-Policy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The policy is ENFORCED in production, and report-only in `npm run dev`.
#
# To go back to report-only in production — for example while investigating a
# wallet that reports a blocked script — set this to 0 (or false/off). Nothing is
# blocked then, and the browser console lists what *would* be.
#
# Before enforcing a change to the policy, open the app in development (where it
# is already report-only) and do a full pass — connect both ways, scan, preview,
# sign, Payload, The Void — with the console open. See "The policy the page now
# sends" in the README.
#
# 1/true forces enforcement anywhere; 0/false/off forces report-only; blank keeps
# the default for the environment.
CSP_ENFORCE=